@IsTest
private class ErpSyncServiceTest {

    private class MockOk implements HttpCalloutMock {
        public HttpResponse respond(HttpRequest req) {
            HttpResponse res = new HttpResponse();
            res.setStatusCode(200);
            res.setBody('{"erpId":"ERP-99"}');
            return res;
        }
    }

    @IsTest
    static void syncsAccountSuccessfully() {
        Account a = new Account(Name = 'Acme');
        insert a;

        Test.setMock(HttpCalloutMock.class, new MockOk());

        Test.startTest();
        ErpSyncService.syncAccounts(new Set<Id>{ a.Id });
        Test.stopTest();          // future fires synchronously here

        Account result = [SELECT ERP_Id__c, Sync_Status__c FROM Account WHERE Id = :a.Id];
        System.assertEquals('ERP-99', result.ERP_Id__c);
        System.assertEquals('Success', result.Sync_Status__c);
    }
}

For the mixed DML test, you need System.runAs() to create the test User — otherwise the test itself hits mixed DML while setting up data:

@IsTest
private class PortalOnboardingServiceTest {

    @IsTest
    static void assignsPermSetWithoutMixedDml() {

        User u;
        // runAs resets the transaction context — test-only escape hatch.
        System.runAs(new User(Id = UserInfo.getUserId())) {
            u = new User(
                Username  = 'test' + DateTime.now().getTime() + '@example.com',
                Email     = 'test@example.com',
                LastName  = 'Test',
                Alias     = 'tst',
                ProfileId = [SELECT Id FROM Profile WHERE Name = 'Standard User'].Id,
                TimeZoneSidKey = 'GMT',
                LocaleSidKey = 'en_US',
                EmailEncodingKey = 'UTF-8',
                LanguageLocaleKey = 'en_US'
            );
            insert u;
        }

        Test.startTest();
        PortalOnboardingService.onboard(
            new Contact(LastName = 'Portal User'), u.Id, 'Portal_Access'
        );
        Test.stopTest();

        System.assertEquals(1, [
            SELECT COUNT() FROM PermissionSetAssignment
            WHERE AssigneeId = :u.Id
            AND PermissionSet.Name = 'Portal_Access'
        ]);
    }
}

One note on the mixed DML case: @future works, but you get no job ID, so nothing tells you the permission set assignment silently failed. A Queueable doing the same split returns an ID you can poll against AsyncApexJob, and it accepts the Contact sObject directly instead of forcing the re-query. Unless you specifically need the trigger-friendly one-liner, Queueable is the better shape for onboarding flows where you care whether the second half landed.

